module in-trial-court-criminal.criminal

// Criminal cases in the Indiana trial courts, from the defendant's side.
// Every period below is stated in a note, not counted: Criminal Rule 4.1
// counts every day of a Rule 4 period, Saturdays, Sundays and holidays
// included, and runs a period ending on a day the court is closed to the
// next day it is open, but lit/core has no counting rule for it or for the
// Appellate Rules. The Indiana Code's criminal-procedure provisions the
// rules refer to (the initial-hearing advisements of Ind. Code
// § 35-33-7-5, the charging requirements of § 35-40-5-12 and the motion
// to dismiss of § 35-34-1-4, among others) are not vendored and stay
// holes. The earlier outline's leading decisions (County of Riverside v.
// McLaughlin, Gerstein v. Pugh, Gideon v. Wainwright, Brady v. Maryland,
// Mapp v. Ohio, Barker v. Wingo, Boykin v. Alabama, Missouri v. Frye,
// Jackson v. Virginia, Ramos v. Louisiana, Apprendi v. New Jersey, Roe v.
// Flores-Ortega, Garza v. Idaho) are removed: no reporter text is
// vendored.
start charged

state charged: "Information or indictment filed" by office cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.1"
{
  note "A criminal case is commenced by filing a charging information or indictment of sufficient specificity to allow preparation of a defense, complying with Ind. Code § 35-40-5-12 (not vendored) and Access to Court Records Rule 5(C) (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.1). Before charges are filed the prosecutor may use a precharge subpoena duces tecum, which the recipient may meet or move to quash within ten days (Ind. Crim. Rule 1.4(A)). The state's appearance states the information Rule 1.3(A) lists. Arrest and the initial hearing's timing are governed by Ind. Code ch. 35-33 (not vendored)."
  edge-charged-initial-hearing "Initial hearing" -> initial-hearing {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.3"
  }
}

decision initial-hearing: "Initial hearing and pretrial release" by court cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.3"
{
  note "At the initial hearing the court advises the defendant of all applicable constitutional rights and deadlines under Ind. Code § 35-33-7-5 (not vendored) and enters a preliminary plea of not guilty unless the defendant enters a different plea; the state may not negotiate a plea with an unrepresented defendant who has not waived counsel, and on defense counsel's request the court may waive the hearing (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.3). An arrestee who presents no substantial risk of flight or danger should be released without money bail or surety on conditions, except an arrestee charged with murder or treason, on pretrial release for another incident, or on probation, parole or community supervision; the court uses an approved evidence-based risk assessment and may set money bail, surety or cash, or a partial cash payment (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.6(A)-(C)). A motion for a change of judge (for bias or prejudice) or of venue from the county is filed within thirty days after the initial hearing, or after first appearing following a remand for new trial; later only on subsequently discovered grounds with the affidavit Rule 2.4(C)(2) requires, and any opposing party has ten days to file counter-affidavits (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.4(A)-(C))."
  edge-initial-hearing-detained "Held on money bail or not released" -> discovery {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.6(C)"
  }
  edge-initial-hearing-released "Released without money bail on conditions" -> discovery {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.6(A)"
  }
}

state discovery: "Discovery and pretrial motions" by applicant cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.5"
{
  note "The state discloses the witnesses, statements, reports, exculpatory material and other items of Rule 2.5(B)(2) within thirty days from the initial hearing, an appearance by defense counsel or an appearance by a pro se defendant, whichever is later; the defense furnishes the items of Rule 2.5(C)(1) within thirty days after the prosecutor's disclosure; disclosure continues as supplemental discovery arises (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.5(A)(4), (B)(1), (C)(1)). A subpoena duces tecum is not served on a non-party until at least fifteen days after the proposed subpoena is served on the other parties, and its recipient responds or moves to quash within twenty days (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.5(E)). Written motions state specific contentions with cogent reasoning and authority; a motion to dismiss is accompanied by a separate memorandum and meets Ind. Code § 35-34-1-4 (not vendored); counsel is strongly encouraged to file a pretrial motion to suppress at least ten days before a jury trial, but the objection is preserved by a timely objection at trial (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.7). In a misdemeanor case the defendant demands a jury by a written demand not later than ten days before the first scheduled trial date, or the jury is waived unless the defendant had less than fifteen days' notice of the trial date and no advisement of the consequence (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.1(B)(1))."
  edge-discovery-motions "Pretrial motion filed" -> motions-ruling {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.7"
  }
  edge-discovery-speedy "Rule 4 period expired; motion for discharge or dismissal" -> speedy-trial {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 4"
  }
  edge-discovery-plea "Plea negotiations" -> plea-decision {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 3.3(C)"
  }
}

decision speedy-trial: "Criminal Rule 4 remedy" by court cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 4"
{
  note "A defendant detained in jail on a pending charge must be tried within 180 days from the later of the filing of the charge or the arrest, excluding delay caused by the defendant, court congestion or emergency; one held beyond it is released on recognizance but remains subject to the charge (Ind. Crim. Rule 4(A)). A jailed defendant who moves for an early trial must be tried within seventy calendar days from the motion, excluding congestion or emergency delay, unless released or delayed by the defendant's act; held beyond it, the charge is dismissed on the defendant's motion (Ind. Crim. Rule 4(B)). No one may be held to answer a charge for more than one year in aggregate from the later of the filing of the charge or the arrest, excluding delay caused by the defendant, congestion or emergency; held beyond it, the charge is dismissed on motion; the one-year limit does not apply to a retrial (Ind. Crim. Rule 4(C)). On a motion to dismiss the trial may be continued for ninety days, the defendant released without money bail, if the state shows unavailable evidence it diligently sought and can obtain within ninety days; if trial does not follow within the ninety days the charges are dismissed with prejudice (Ind. Crim. Rule 4(D)). Every day is counted, a period ending on a day the court is closed runs to the next open day, a continuance's delay is measured between the trial or hearing dates, and the court designates the cause of each continuance; if the defendant causes delay in the last thirty days of a period the state may petition for an additional thirty days (Ind. Crim. Rule 4.1). For a defendant held out of state, in federal custody or in another county, the periods commence as Rule 4.2 provides."
  edge-speedy-trial-released "Jail limit passed: released on recognizance; the charge continues" -> plea-decision {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 4(A)"
  }
  edge-speedy-trial-dismissed "Early-trial or one-year limit passed: charge dismissed" -> dismissed {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 4(B)"
  }
  edge-speedy-trial-extended "State shows unavailable evidence: trial continued ninety days" -> plea-decision {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 4(D)"
  }
}

decision motions-ruling: "Ruling on pretrial motions" by court cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.7"
{
  note "A motion to dismiss and a motion to suppress are decided on the contentions and authority Rule 2.7 requires; the grounds and timing of a motion to dismiss are set by Ind. Code § 35-34-1-4, which is not vendored (Ind. Crim. Rule 2.7(A))."
  edge-motions-ruling-dismissed "Charges dismissed" -> dismissed {
    authority cite ?("Ind. Code § 35-34-1-4 (motion to dismiss; not vendored)")
  }
  edge-motions-ruling-denied "Motions denied; case proceeds" -> plea-decision {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 2.7"
  }
}

decision plea-decision: "Plea or trial" by applicant cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 3.3(A)"
{
  note "A defendant may plead not guilty, guilty, or guilty but mentally ill (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.3(A)). Before accepting a guilty plea the court advises the defendant of the nature of the charges, the rights waived (including the right to appeal the conviction), the maximum and minimum sentences, the firearm consequence in a domestic-violence case, that an accepted plea agreement binds the court, and the immigration consequences; a felony plea's waiver is oral, a misdemeanor plea's may be written; the court finds the plea voluntary and, before judgment, a factual basis, and a defendant may not plead guilty while asserting innocence (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.3(B)). The court does not take part in plea discussions; a felony plea agreement is written and filed; the court may take it under advisement until sentencing, and if it accepts it is bound by it (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.3(C))."
  edge-plea-decision-plea "Guilty plea accepted" -> sentencing {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 3.3(C)"
  }
  edge-plea-decision-trial "Trial" -> trial { authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 3.1" }
}

decision trial: "Verdict" by court cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 3.4"
{
  note "A defendant charged with a felony is tried by jury unless the defendant waives it personally, knowingly, voluntarily and intelligently, with the consent of the state and the court; in a misdemeanor case a jury is had only on a timely written demand, and the state may not request one (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.1). Jury instructions follow Jury Rules 20 and 26 and Trial Rule 51 (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.2). The court promptly notes the verdict or decision in the chronological case summary and enters judgment of conviction at or before sentencing (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.4)."
  edge-trial-acquittal "Not guilty" -> acquitted { authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 3.4" }
  edge-trial-guilty "Guilty; sentencing" -> sentencing { authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.1(A)" }
}

state sentencing: "Sentencing and advisements" by court cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.1"
{
  note "Upon entering a conviction the court sentences the defendant within thirty days of the plea or the finding or verdict of guilty, unless extended for good cause (Ind. Crim. Rule 5.1(A)). After sentencing the judge immediately advises the defendant of the right to appeal or to move to correct error within thirty days of sentencing, of the thirty-day notice of appeal, and of appointed appellate counsel, and asks whether the defendant wishes to appeal (Ind. Crim. Rule 5.1(B), (C)). On a felony conviction the court completes an electronic abstract of judgment (Ind. Crim. Rule 5.2). Sentencing ranges and the presentence report are set by the Indiana Code (art. 35-38), which is not vendored."
  edge-sentencing-judgment "Sentence entered on the chronological case summary" -> judgment {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.4"
  }
}

decision judgment: "Judgment of conviction" by applicant cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.4"
{
  note "A motion to correct error is not a prerequisite for appeal except to raise newly discovered material evidence, including jury misconduct, producible within thirty days after sentencing; if filed, it is filed within thirty days after the date of sentencing (Ind. Crim. Rule 5.3). The notice of appeal is filed within thirty days after the sentencing order is entered on the chronological case summary, or, after a timely motion to correct error, within thirty days after the ruling is entered or the motion is deemed denied under Trial Rule 53.3, whichever is earlier (Ind. Crim. Rule 5.4; Ind. Appellate Rule 9(A)(1)). Unless the notice of appeal is timely filed, the right to appeal is forfeited except as provided by Post-Conviction Rule 2 (Ind. Appellate Rule 9(A)(5)). lit/core has no counting rule for these rules, so the periods are not encoded as counted deadlines."
  edge-judgment-correct-error "Motion to correct error" -> correct-error {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.3"
  }
  edge-judgment-appeal "Notice of appeal filed" -> appeal-taken {
    authority cite "Ind. Appellate Rule 9(A)"
  }
  edge-judgment-no-appeal "No appeal" -> conviction-final {
    authority cite "Ind. Appellate Rule 9(A)"
  }
}

decision correct-error: "Motion to correct error decided" by court cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.3(B)"
{
  note "The motion is filed within thirty days after the date of sentencing (Ind. Crim. Rule 5.3(B)); the notice of appeal then runs thirty days from the ruling's entry on the chronological case summary or the motion's deemed denial under Trial Rule 53.3, whichever occurs first (Ind. Crim. Rule 5.4; Ind. Appellate Rule 9(A)(1))."
  edge-correct-error-new-trial "Motion granted: new trial" -> trial {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.3(A)"
  }
  edge-correct-error-appeal "Motion denied or deemed denied; notice of appeal filed" -> appeal-taken {
    authority cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 5.4"
  }
  edge-correct-error-no-appeal "Motion denied; no appeal" -> conviction-final {
    authority cite "Ind. Appellate Rule 9(A)"
  }
}

terminal acquitted: "Acquitted" outcome [win] cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 3.4"
{
  note "Outcome from the defendant's side: a verdict or decision of not guilty noted in the chronological case summary (Ind. Crim. Rule 3.4)."
}

terminal dismissed: "Charges dismissed" outcome [win, dismissal] cite "Ind. Crim. Rule 4"
{
  note "Outcome from the defendant's side. A Criminal Rule 4(B) or 4(C) dismissal follows the defendant's motion, and one after a failed ninety-day extension is with prejudice (Ind. Crim. Rule 4(D)); whether any other dismissal bars refiling is set by the Indiana Code, which is not vendored."
}

terminal appeal-taken: "Appeal taken" outcome [appeal] cite "Ind. Appellate Rule 9(A)"
{
  note "The appeal continues before the Indiana Court of Appeals, or the Supreme Court where the Appellate Rules give it jurisdiction, under the Appellate Rules (sources/indctapp; lit/indctapp)."
}

terminal conviction-final: "Conviction final" outcome [loss] cite ?("Ind. Post-Conviction Rule 1 (post-conviction relief; not vendored)") {
  note "Outcome from the defendant's side. Post-conviction relief under Post-Conviction Rule 1 and a belated appeal under Post-Conviction Rule 2 are not vendored; federal habeas belongs to lit/habeas."
}
